669 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 436 0 R
/E (A JWT has three parts, header, payload, and signature, separated by dots. The signature is a cryptographic proof that the token was issued by a trusted authority \(in this case, AWS Cognito\) and has not been tampered with. Any server that accepts JWTs as authentication credentials must verify the signature on every request; decoding the payload alone is not authentication, it is merely parsing.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
670 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 436 0 R
/E (DVSA-ORDER-MANAGER extracts the Authorization header, splits the token at the dots, base64-decodes only the middle section \(the payload\), and reads the username field from it. The signature section is never inspected. This means any attacker who can construct a JSON object with a target user's UUID and base64-encode it can impersonate that user, the cryptographic proof of identity is treated as decoration rather than a security control.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
