/T (In serverless architectures, Lambda functions can execute in parallel, and without strict server-side sequencing, an attacker can exploit the time delay between a check \(status verification\) and an action \(updating an order\).)
/E (In serverless architectures, Lambda functions can execute in parallel, and without strict server-side sequencing, an attacker can exploit the time delay between a check \(status verification\) and an action \(updating an order\).)
/Pg 86 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
1456 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 502 0 R
/T (This lesson also highlights that simply checking a value is not enough; you must "lock" the state of a resource as soon as a sensitive workflow, such as billing, begins.)
/E (This lesson also highlights that simply checking a value is not enough; you must "lock" the state of a resource as soon as a sensitive workflow, such as billing, begins.)
/Pg 86 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
1457 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 502 0 R
/T (In addition, a security-critical threshold \(like > 110\) should never leave room for an "in-between" state \(like 100\) to be treated as valid for updates. The fix demonstrates that moving to >= 100 immediately closes the vulnerability window.)
/E (In addition, a security-critical threshold \(like > 110\) should never leave room for an "in-between" state \(like 100\) to be treated as valid for updates. The fix demonstrates that moving to >= 100 immediately closes the vulnerability window.)
/Pg 86 0 R
/K [2]
>>
